  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 PM EDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nTraining showers and thunderstorms have been causing flooding as \nexpected today. The flash flood watch will be allowed to continue\ntoday over the northern two thirds of the area, with the southeast\ncorner of the current watch extended into Thursday per discussion\nbelow.\n\n\nThe summertime cold front currently over our northwest counties will \nsag slowly south and east the remainder of today and tonight. \nExactly how far it makes it before becoming nearly stationary is not \ncertain, but right now it looks like it will be around or just south \nof our south/southeast border by sometime Thursday morning.  As the \nfront shifts south, the threat of heavy rainfall will shift south as \nwell. The atmosphere will still be primed for heavy rain rates with \ntall skinny CAPE profiles and high PWAT values around or above 2 \ninches across the south ahead of the front, and the front will still \nbe the focus for showers and possibly a few storms tonight into \nThursday. Storm motions roughly parallel to the front will also \ncontinue the threat of training cells. CAMS and the hi-res ensembles \nshow a band of risk for very heavy rain across our SW NC and \nsoutheast counties. The flash flood guidance is generally higher \nsouth than it was further north, but given the potential for \ntraining of high efficiency rainmakers will issue an additional \nflash flood watch for tonight into Thursday for our southeastern \ncounties and will also extend the southeast corner of the current\nwatch into Thursday. Northwestern areas should be dry Thursday \nbehind the front.\n\n\nThe remainder of the forecast has the basic pattern of upper ridging \nto our west and troughing over the eastern CONUS continuing.  We \nwill see short wave energy moving southeast on the back side of the \ntrough at times that will bring additional showers and storms to our \narea for the Friday through Wednesday period.  Timing of these \nfeatures and the resulting convection is still uncertain especially \nas we get further out in time. However, for Friday and Saturday with \nthe front not far from our southern border, the chances for showers \nand storms will be highest south along with the threat of locally \nheavy rainfall and flooding. The location of greatest flooding \nthreat will depend on exactly where the front is located which is \nstill unknown. Temperatures for Thursday into Saturday look to be a \nbit below normal, then the remainder of the period through Wednesday \nwill likely see temperatures not far from normal for this time of \nyear.",
